Private Maxima bindings in parallel computations
================================================

The appended tests in rtest_mset.mac load parallel-bindings-check.lisp.
Run the complete file using the usual harness:

  timeout 180 ./maxima-local --no-init \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_mset"]);'

WITH-PRIVATE-MAXIMA-BINDINGS surrounds MBIND, the body, and MUNBIND in
MLAMBDA and MBINDING-SUB. Arguments and initializers keep their existing
order of evaluation. While evaluating a parallel item, symbol value cells
are bound with PROGV, preserving both inherited values and unboundness.
Ordinary serial evaluation uses no additional symbol bindings. The macro
keeps the body inline, including lexical GO/RETURN and multiple values.

A nested worker captures active local variables and the enclosing runner's
iteration variables. Builtin list assignment may bind nested lists of
symbols. Custom assignment targets can modify arrays, records or arbitrary
shared state; parallel local bindings reject them before invoking their
assignment extension. Ordinary serial bindings keep their existing behavior.

VALUES and MYOPTIONS have private list storage in each runner. Otherwise
registering or removing a temporary local would mutate another runner's
list. When the runner unwinds, its nonlocal registration/removal effects
are merged into its captured parent, including removal followed by
reassignment and additions from other completed runners. Historical
MYOPTIONS entries are retained. A short lock protects snapshots and merges;
mathematical evaluation holds no registry lock. A captured local variable's
registration does not escape into the parent's VALUES. Nonlocal variable
values themselves retain the existing assignment behavior.

Tests cover bound/unbound parameter and block cells, explicit nested worker
capture, direct and nested list binding, argument order, duplicate/rest/
quoted parameters, multiple bodies, ASSIGN and setter hooks, argument-count
and partial-binding failures, errors/THROW/GO/RETURN, and multiple values.
The symbol-macro cases verify that a constant-valued macro is not treated
as a constant variable and that binding does not execute its expander.
A fixed generator compares 128 streams of 20 registry operations against
an independent append/delete model. Synchronized native workers overlap on
the same variable and independently update registry entries. Their waits
are bounded and threads are joined during cleanup. Threadless runtimes
exercise portable scopes and the serial fallback. End-to-end examples use
ordinary Maxima lambdas and the public parallel_makelist scheduler.

This is not a guarantee for every infolist or shared data structure.
FUNCTIONS, arrays, symbol properties, assumptions and other shared objects
retain their own restrictions. A body must still be independent of other
bodies; private local cells do not make intentional writes to the same
nonlocal variable safe.

A separate THROW probe exits the whole runner and checks that additions,
removals and option registrations still reach the parent. Ordinary item errors
are caught inside RUN-ONE-ITEM and do not exercise that unwinding path. The
probe covers direct scope, caller, worker and serial fallback execution.
